<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cairo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cairo is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, founded in 969 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> as their capital, next to the older settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>. It was the seat of the Fatimid Caliphate, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, and after 1261 hosted a line of Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> protection. Cairo became one of the largest and wealthiest cities of the medieval Islamic world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cairo.jpg" alt="Cairo" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jorge Láscar from Melbourne, Australia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cairo_Opera_House,_Al_Hurriyah_Park_and_the_Nile_river_(14797782354).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Even after the Mongol destruction of Baghdad in 1258, the shadow caliphate maintained by the Mamluks in <b>Cairo</b> (1261–1517) gave legitimacy to Muslim rulers across North Africa and the Levant.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He was a Kurdish commander of exceptional political intelligence who began as a constrained vizier in <b>Cairo</b> and became the master of the largest Muslim realm between the Nile and the Tigris.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From the accession of Barquq in 1382 until 1517, most sultans of Mamluk Egypt and Syria were Circassians who had been brought to <b>Cairo</b> as slave soldiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cairo</b> was founded north of Fustat as a palatine capital, and the al-Azhar mosque was begun in 970.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The alliance with Mamluk Egypt supplied Kipchak recruits to the Mamluk army and kept a diplomatic channel open between the steppe and <b>Cairo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…law, and Ibn Khaldun, born in Tunis in 1332 to a family of Andalusi origin, received his education there, served several Hafsid rulers and returned to teach in the city in the 1370s and 1380s before leaving for <b>Cairo</b>, where he completed the work that included his Muqaddimah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
